Z||000000||MR2KLR||12-14-2001||11:01 AM||||<BR>This is the first time I EVER broke off a bolt in my long life of working on my fiero's, I'm 20 and on my 3rd Fiero in 4 years ain't bad (Right?) So I'm changing out the alt. in my 88 Coupe I was trying to get the Bracket of that runs from the back of the Alt. to the back or the Intake manifold with out takeing apart any of the intake So I decided to unbolt the bracket from the manifold and to remove the bracket from the Alt. out of the car. well just as I was thinking about just how much weight I was puting on my fragile manifold the head of the bolt came flying off with great force.<P>I realy don't want to remove intake manfold anytime soon. is that braket needed, I'll have it fixed by spring, But would it be ok just for now?<P>------------------<BR>  <BR>Black 88 Coupe: 120 MPH spedo, Aux. gauge pod, 195/55VR14 Potenza RE-71 tires, GT exhaust & ground F/X Soon to have 16" White Beretta GTZ W/ 215/45ZR16's<BR><A HREF="http://community-2.webtv.net/TOMTOM81/MYNEWTOY/" TARGET=_blank>Tom's Page</A>||||reg||5
Z||000001||Turbo88||12-14-2001||01:42 PM||||First of all, don't worry about breaking a bolt. It's a pain in the ass but it is something I am willing to bet everyone on this board has done at one point or another. <P>I can't picture what you are describing as the location of the broken bolt but, if you can get a drill to it to drill a hole in the middle of it, go to Sears, buy an EZ Out kit and follow the instructions. No problem.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000002||MR2KLR||12-14-2001||02:51 PM||||I know about E-Z outs BUT I'll need to remove the intake manifold from the car to get at it. The bolt I broke off is a 13mm bolt on the Rear right corner of a 87-88 Duke (serpentine belt) it mounts a 3" long bent steel rod to the top rear (engine side) center. It seems to be a reinforcement for the Alternator.||||reg||1
